- op: discuss  # db2f
  at: '2026-08-12T07:51:32Z'  # db2f
  by: agent  # db2f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # db2f
  clock: 3  # db2f
  nonce: db2f  # db2f
  message: "SATISFIED. The gate now asks whether the CALL was examined, not whether the\nmodule's name was recognised.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE — this item's own filed repro re-run against the fixed tree,\nplus four more of the same shape found while working it, plus a guard in the\nother direction. Same fixtures, same claims file, shipped CLI, no overlay, no\nflags, no third-party dependency:\n\n  fixture                                          claim      before        after\n  -----------------------------------------------  ---------  ------------  --------------\n  telnetlib.Telnet(host,23); conn.write(secret)     net_send   confirmed 0   inconclusive 2\n  ssl.create_default_context(); sock.sendall(s)     net_send   confirmed 0   inconclusive 2\n  ctypes.CDLL(\"libc.so.6\").system(b\"curl ...\")      net_send   confirmed 0   inconclusive 2\n  os.sendfile(sock_fd, file_fd, 0, 4096)            net_send   confirmed 0   inconclusive 2\n  os.open(p,O_WRONLY|O_CREAT); os.write(fd,data)    fs_write   confirmed 0   inconclusive 2\n  CONTROL requests.post(url, data=secret)           net_send   inconclusive  inconclusive 2\n  CONTROL os.makedirs(p); os.remove(p)              fs_write   violated 1    violated 1\n  CONTROL with open(..) as fh: json.dump(obj, fh)   net_send   confirmed 0   confirmed 0\n\nThe last row is the guard in the other direction, and it is why the fix is not a\nblanket downgrade: a program whose external calls the catalogue CAN classify\nstill confirms. The same fixture correctly reports `violated` for fs_write.\n\nPost-fix reason on the filed repro, verbatim: \"No net_send chains found, but the\nanalysis makes calls into 2 module(s) that the I/O catalog could not classify\n(os, telnetlib), so whether those calls perform this I/O was never examined.\"\n\n*** THE FIX IS NOT THE ONE THIS ITEM PROPOSED, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS. ***\nThe item framed the answer as swapping the module-level predicate:\nis_stdlib_module -> is_stdlib_module_complete. I built that first. FIVE\nINDEPENDENT ADVERSARIAL REVIEWERS REFUTED IT, 5 of 5 fatal, and they were right:\n\n  A MODULE-LEVEL ANSWER IS WRONG AT BOTH ENDS. It called `os` unexamined while\n  the same run was classifying os.mkdir through it. Measured: a fixture calling\n  json.dump(obj, fh) printed \"no I/O catalog coverage (builtins, json)\" one line\n  above \"2 fs_write chain(s) found\" -- chains found through those very modules.\n  It also broke three existing tests, one of them the project's own named guard\n  against the blanket-downgrade failure mode.\n\n  THE UNIT OF EXAMINATION IS THE CALL SITE. A call the catalogue CLASSIFIED was\n  examined; that is what examination is. The enumeration record settles only the\n  calls it could NOT classify, which is a strictly smaller question. Under that\n  rule all three broken tests pass UNMODIFIED, which is the strongest evidence\n  available that they were right and the first draft wrong.\n\nSo the shipped rule is, in order: (1) the catalogue classified this call ->\nexamined; (2) else the module carries a dated closed-world audit -> examined\nnegative; (3) else the callee's source was read this run -> first-party, not a\ncatalogue gap; (4) else block. Branches (1) and (2) replace the two that failed\nopen: is_stdlib_module (name recognition -- 283 of python's 300 stdlib modules\ncarry no row) and row PRESENCE (boundary-blind and surface-blind -- `os` has 40\nrows, none of them os.open/os.write/os.sendfile, and modules with filesystem-only\nrows vouched for NETWORK claims).\n\nMATCHING IS EXACT, not prefix. A draft let a declaration descend a separator;\nthat is wrong in three shipped languages at once -- `urllib` is a namespace\npackage whose submodules have unrelated I/O surfaces, Go's `/` is not containment\n(a declaration for `os` would have vouched for `os/exec`), and Rust's `::` was\nnot in the separator list at all.\n\nONE HOME PER QUESTION, which is the plan's binding requirement for this bucket:\n`io_boundary.classify_call` answers \"did the catalogue classify this call\" for\nBOTH the tagging pass and the gate -- they were about to disagree, and that\ndisagreement was the defect. `is_stdlib_module_complete` is folded into\n`module_io_is_enumerated` so the coverage gate and the F3 Filter 2 skip read one\nclosed-world predicate. A parity test reads the three callers' own source via\n`inspect` and fails if any grows a private copy.\n\nCOST, measured, six repos / six languages, with production's own\n_uncatalogued_external_modules as the control:\n\n  repo         distinct modules   blocked BEFORE   blocked AFTER   verdict change\n  httpx                     71               27              65    none (already inconclusive)\n  fastapi                   69               50              64    none (already inconclusive)\n  poetry                   196              127             192    none (already inconclusive)\n  hypergumbo               204              104             198    none (already inconclusive)\n  pretix                   420              329             410    none (already inconclusive)\n  django                   269               75             241    none (already inconclusive)\n\nZERO verdict changes -- but that is 0-OF-6, not a claim about the population. A\nwider corpus was not run, and a reviewer reports 2 would-confirm -> inconclusive\nflips on a 60-repo sweep that this cohort is too small to have seen. What makes\nthe strict direction affordable is structural rather than lucky: verify_claim\nreturns `violated` OUTSIDE the coverage branch, so coverage gates only the\nall-clear and can never suppress a detection.\n\nBLAST RADIUS BEYOND BOUNDARY CLAIMS, disclosed because nothing in the framing\nimplies it: `_taint_blind_reason` deliberately reuses compute_boundary_coverage\nrather than growing a second \"did this language produce call edges\"\nimplementation, so this tightens every taint_flow verdict too.\n\nALSO FOUND AND FIXED HERE, each with a control:\n\n- The mirror arm this item reported but had NOT re-run is REAL. A Go program with\n  no filesystem I/O at all returns inconclusive naming `fmt`, `sort`, `strings`.\n  The same predicate over-blocked 13 languages and under-blocked python, and\n  which one you got depended on whether `stdlib_modules` had been populated --\n  which happened for python as part of a SUPPLY-CHAIN feature, not for claim\n  verification. Now uniform, pinned by a parity test over the live registry.\n\n- An overlay could relabel a PyPI package as stdlib. load_overlay_catalog pops\n  `stdlib_modules` precisely to prevent this (ADR-0016: \"would be a supply-chain\n  misread rather than an I/O one\"), but _from_dict auto-promoted every\n  stdlib_module_completeness entry into the same set. Control: the same overlay\n  spelling it `stdlib_modules:` was correctly stripped. ADR-0041 §3's normative\n  single-source paragraph is amended to match.\n\nWHAT IS STILL OPEN: an audit record exists for exactly one module (`math`), so a\nprogram touching a call the catalogue cannot classify needs that module audited\nbefore it can confirm. Filed separately with the measured backlog. The reason\nstring is also capped at 5 names sorted alphabetically, so a stdlib module can\nevict the third-party library a reader most needs -- filed."  # db2f
